Scene integrity is non-negotiable: Use an abstract non-embodied composition only: the page uses editorial paper structures rather than embodied subjects..

Use comparison as the one dominant visual grammar, never a second grammar. Its required topology is: Make two or more source-supported alternatives visibly different even when labels are hidden; at least one difference must be object, position, connection, sequence, or boundary.. It must visibly prove: two or more alternatives; visible structural difference. Generic symbols, anonymous nodes, or arrows alone do not prove the source relationship: every non-self-evident source step or term must be identified by the exact short label or note declared in this page's annotation plan. Do not substitute: single-process diagram; name-only comparison; generic chart.

Make this selected grammar visibly provable in the finished image through these reader-visible facts: the article sheet is one continuous structure with an inline explanation; the presentation side is visibly split into distinct frame units; the forms differ through object structure and placement, not labels alone. Each proof must be visible in the content stage itself, not supplied only by the title or subtitle. Do not let one generic illustrative scene substitute for this evidence.

Make these source-supported comparison differences visibly distinct in objects, positions, connections, sequence, or boundaries: article delivery is one continuous reading surface with an inline insertion; presentation delivery is a set of separate ordered frames.

